  "summary_md": "Today’s leisure and semiconductor batch splits between genuine operational momentum and speculative repositioning. AMBQ stands out: ultra-low-power edge AI chips are decoupled from broader semiconductor weakness, with management consistently overdelivering and raising fresh capital from a position of strength. In leisure, FNKO’s turnaround is hitting a new gear—record margins and lower leverage are driving earnings expansion even on modest revenue, validating its shift from volume-chasing to value-focused execution. The most radical strategic event is AGH’s planned reverse-merger, abandoning legacy golf-course operations to become an NDAA-compliant counter-drone manufacturer—a binary leap from asset liquidation to defense start‑up.\n\nA cross‑cutting pattern is heavy insider selling at momentum names: QUIK rallies on sector euphoria despite zero insider purchases and consistent executive selling, while PLBY removes a major overhang by buying back a large legacy stake at a discount, adding credibility to its brand-licensing pivot. Multiple semiconductor firms are pivoting toward defense and AI infrastructure—PRSO’s Israeli drone identification win, INDI’s new ADAS system‑on‑chip, IPWR’s B‑TRAN for data centers—but commercialization remains early and dilution risks are widespread.",
